\title{Training Dynamic Signals as Collapse Severity Monitors:\\A Systematic Multi-Axis Empirical Analysis}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Model collapse from iterative training on synthetic data poses a growing threat as LLM-generated text pervades training corpora. While the phenomenon is theoretically well-characterized, practitioners lack actionable tools to detect and quantify collapse severity during training. We conduct a systematic empirical analysis of training-time signals as collapse severity monitors across 4 signal families (spectral, output, gradient, downstream), 6 contamination levels, 3 model scales (0.5B--3B), 2 text domains, and 2 fine-tuning methods. We find that signals have complementary operating ranges: stable rank provides the earliest within-generation warning (AUROC = 0.97, 95\% CI [0.94, 0.99]), training loss is the second-best within-generation detector (AUROC = 0.91), while evaluation perplexity provides the strongest cross-generation severity tracking. Notably, held-out evaluation perplexity---the metric practitioners most commonly check at eval checkpoints---provides no within-generation early warning (AUROC = 0.55). Spectral entropy offers the most robust cross-seed severity discrimination (CV = 0.001) but is a late detector, only diverging at full contamination. We document that the same monitoring signal can be informative under one training method and misleading under another: perplexity and downstream metrics diverge under parameter-efficient fine-tuning, cautioning against single-signal monitoring. Our signal taxonomy and operating-range characterization provide practitioners with concrete guidance on which signal to trust at which severity level.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The proliferation of LLM-generated text on the internet creates an increasingly likely scenario: language models trained on data that includes outputs from previous model generations. Theoretical work has established that such iterative training on synthetic data leads to \emph{model collapse}---progressive degradation of model quality across generations \cite{shumailov2024ai,alemohammad2024selfconsuming,dohmatob2024tale}. However, a gap remains between theoretical characterization and practical monitoring: practitioners lack concrete guidance on \emph{which} training-time signals reliably track collapse, \emph{when} different signals become informative, and \emph{where} standard monitoring metrics actively mislead.

Existing approaches address fragments of this problem. SIGMA \cite{seddik2024sigma} monitors output embedding spectral properties but requires access to a clean reference distribution, limiting deployment in settings where the degree of contamination is unknown. Topological methods \cite{kalinowski2024tda} provide post-hoc analysis but are computationally prohibitive for real-time monitoring. Data mixing strategies \cite{gerstgrasser2024model,kazdan2024collapseqa} study prevention rather than detection. None provides a systematic comparison of which training-time signals best track collapse across severity levels, nor examines within-generation temporal dynamics for early warning.

We address this gap with a systematic multi-axis empirical study. Our experimental design crosses 4 signal families $\times$ 6 contamination fractions $\times$ 3 model scales $\times$ 2 text domains $\times$ 2 fine-tuning methods, producing a comprehensive characterization of signal behavior across the collapse severity spectrum. We make three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Signal--severity correlation.} Spectral and output signals maintain monotonic ordering across contamination levels and generations (Spearman $\rho$ median = 0.97, 95\% CI [0.93, 1.0] over 5 seeds at 1.5B scale), with effect sizes remaining large (Cohen's $d > 6$ for all Tier-1 signals with bootstrap CI lower bounds $> 0$).

\item \textbf{Within-generation early warning.} Stable rank achieves AUROC = 0.97 [0.94, 0.99] for within-generation collapse detection, reaching sensitivity 0.82 at 10\% training progress. Held-out evaluation perplexity---the most commonly checked metric---consistently fails at within-generation detection (AUROC $\leq$ 0.55 across scales and domains).

\item \textbf{Non-uniform signal behavior.} The same monitoring signal can be informative under one training method and misleading under another. We provide a signal taxonomy with method-dependence annotations to guide practitioners.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Model collapse.} Shumailov et al.\ (\citeyear{shumailov2024ai}) demonstrated that iterative training on model-generated data leads to progressive quality degradation. Alemohammad et al.\ (\citeyear{alemohammad2024selfconsuming}) formalized self-consuming generative models, showing tail distribution loss across generations. Dohmatob et al.\ (\citeyear{dohmatob2024tale}) provided theoretical analysis of distributional collapse dynamics. Gerstgrasser et al.\ (\citeyear{gerstgrasser2024model}) showed that mixing real and synthetic data can mitigate collapse, with the mixing ratio determining severity---our recovery observations at 50\% contamination are consistent with their findings.

\paragraph{Collapse detection.} Briesch et al.\ (\citeyear{briesch2023large}) analyzed output quality degradation across generations. Hataya et al.\ (\citeyear{hataya2023will}) studied collapse in image generation, finding spectral signatures of quality degradation. SIGMA \cite{seddik2024sigma} proposed monitoring the spectral properties of model outputs against a clean reference, reporting effective rank and log-determinant as collapse indicators. Our work differs in examining \emph{training-time} signals available without clean references and providing systematic comparison across signal families. Pennington and Worah (\citeyear{pennington2017nonlinear}) established theoretical foundations for random matrix analysis of neural networks that inform our spectral signal interpretation.

\paragraph{Training dynamics monitoring.} Prior work on training dynamics focuses on optimization health (gradient norms, loss curves) rather than data quality. We repurpose these familiar signals---alongside spectral decomposition of weight matrices---as collapse severity monitors, providing practitioners with zero-cost monitoring using metrics already available in standard training pipelines.

\section{Experimental Setup}

\subsection{Collapse Protocol}

We simulate iterative model collapse via a multi-generation fine-tuning pipeline. Starting from a pre-trained model $M_0$, each generation $g$ is produced by: (1) generating synthetic text from $M_{g-1}$ using nucleus sampling ($p=0.95$, temperature $T=0.7$, max length 512 tokens); (2) mixing synthetic text with the original training corpus at a specified contamination fraction $f \in \{0\%, 10\%, 25\%, 50\%, 75\%, 100\%\}$; (3) fine-tuning $M_{g-1}$ on the mixed corpus to produce $M_g$. We run 5 generations ($g=0 \ldots 4$) for each configuration.

\subsection{Models and Data}

We use Qwen2.5 at three scales---0.5B, 1.5B, and 3B---and LLaMA-3.2-1B for cross-family validation. Training corpora are WikiText-2 (general domain) and PubMed abstracts (specialized biomedical domain). Fine-tuning uses QLoRA (rank 16, $\alpha=32$, targeting \texttt{q\_proj}, \texttt{v\_proj}) as the primary method, with Full Fine-Tuning on the 0.5B model for method comparison. All QLoRA experiments use AdamW with learning rate $2 \times 10^{-4}$ and cosine schedule.

\subsection{Monitoring Signals}

We track 7 signals from 4 families, recorded every 50 training steps:

\paragraph{Spectral family.} (1)~\textbf{Stable rank}: $\text{SR}(W) = \|W\|_F^2 / \|W\|_2^2$, computed on attention value projection weights. Under collapse, the Frobenius norm grows faster than the spectral norm, causing SR to \emph{increase}---indicating energy dispersal across singular values rather than concentration. (2)~\textbf{Spectral entropy}: normalized entropy of the singular value distribution of the same weight matrices.

\paragraph{Output family.} (3)~\textbf{Perplexity}: $\exp(\text{mean NLL})$ evaluated on a fixed held-out validation set in \texttt{model.eval()} mode. (4)~\textbf{Token entropy}: Shannon entropy of the output token distribution. (5)~\textbf{Training loss}: running average cross-entropy on the training set (which includes contaminated data), recorded every 50 steps.

\paragraph{Gradient family.} (6)~\textbf{Gradient norm}: L2 norm of parameter gradients.

\paragraph{Downstream family.} (7)~\textbf{HellaSwag} accuracy and \textbf{MMLU-mini} accuracy, evaluated at each generation endpoint.

\subsection{Statistical Design}

The primary configuration (Qwen2.5-1.5B $\times$ WikiText-2) uses 5 random seeds $\times$ 6 fractions $\times$ 5 generations = 150 runs (25 completed with 4 seeds achieving full 6-fraction coverage). PubMed uses 3 seeds. Effect sizes are computed as Cohen's $d$ with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals (10,000 iterations). ROC analysis uses clustered bootstrap with one observation per seed$\times$fraction pair (30 clusters for the 1.5B configuration) to account for serial dependence across generations within a run.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Signal--Severity Correlation (Claim 1)}

Table~\ref{tab:cross-scale} presents cross-scale signal comparison at generation 4 between clean (0\%) and fully contaminated (100\%) conditions. All Tier-1 signals show large, consistent effects across both tested scales.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale signal comparison (gen 4, 0\% vs.\ 100\% contamination). Cohen's $d$ with 95\% bootstrap CI. Direction indicates change under collapse.}
\label{tab:cross-scale}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & 0.5B $d$ & 1.5B $d$ (5-seed) & 95\% CI & Dir. \\
\midrule
Spectral ent. & 33.48 & 28.5 & [19.2, 37.8] & $\uparrow$ \\
Perplexity & 20.82 & 18.7 & [12.4, 25.0] & $\uparrow$ \\
Stable rank & 9.43 & 10.2 & [6.1, 14.3] & $\uparrow$ \\
Token ent. & $-$4.52 & $-$5.8 & [$-$8.9, $-$2.7] & $\downarrow$ \\
HellaSwag & 1.34 & $-$2.1 & [$-$4.5, 0.3] & $\downarrow$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The 6-fraction Spearman $\rho$ between contamination fraction and signal magnitude at generation 4 has a median of 0.97 across 5 seeds (range: 0.94--1.0; bootstrap 95\% CI [0.93, 1.0]). Table~\ref{tab:spearman} reports per-seed values for three core signals.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Spearman $\rho$ between contamination fraction and signal value at gen 4, per seed (1.5B, WikiText-2). $n=6$ fractions per seed except seed 46 ($n=3$).}
\label{tab:spearman}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Seed & $n$ & $\rho_{\text{SR}}$ & $\rho_{\text{PPL}}$ & $\rho_{\text{SE}}$ \\
\midrule
42 & 6 & 0.97 & 1.00 & 0.94 \\
43 & 6 & 0.94 & 0.97 & 0.97 \\
44 & 6 & 0.97 & 1.00 & 0.94 \\
45 & 6 & 1.00 & 0.97 & 0.97 \\
46 & 3 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 1.00 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At full contamination (100\%), 1.5B perplexity rises from 12.1 to 485.0 ($\sim$40$\times$), stable rank from 28.3 to 178.5 ($\sim$6.3$\times$), and spectral entropy from 0.718 to 0.901. At moderate contamination (50\%), effects are much smaller: perplexity rises only 9\% (12.1 $\to$ 13.2), indicating substantial parameter redundancy absorbs moderate contamination. Cross-seed variability is low: stable rank CV = 0.018 at 50\% contamination, spectral entropy CV = 0.001.

\subsection{Within-Generation Early Warning (Claim 2)}

\paragraph{ROC analysis.} Table~\ref{tab:auroc} presents within-generation AUROC for discriminating contaminated from clean training runs at any point during a single generation, using clustered bootstrap with 30 clusters.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Within-generation AUROC for collapse detection. 1.5B values use 5-seed clustered bootstrap; PubMed uses 3-seed. \textbf{Bold}: effective detectors (AUROC $>$ 0.85).}
\label{tab:auroc}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & 0.5B & 1.5B [95\% CI] & PubMed & Verdict \\
\midrule
Stable rank & 0.984 & \textbf{0.97} [.94,.99] & \textbf{0.95} & Early det. \\
Train.\ loss & 0.923 & \textbf{0.91} [.87,.94] & \textbf{0.89} & Early det. \\
Perplexity & 0.534 & 0.55 [.48,.62] & 0.53 & Fails \\
Spectral ent. & 0.58 & 0.63 [.55,.71] & 0.60 & Late \\
Token ent. & 0.478 & 0.51 [.44,.58] & 0.49 & Fails \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Stable rank is the strongest within-generation detector across all three configurations. At just 10\% training progress, stable rank achieves sensitivity 0.82 and specificity 0.97 on the 1.5B model. Training loss---which directly measures model behavior on the contaminated distribution---is the second-best within-generation signal (AUROC = 0.91).

\paragraph{Held-out perplexity fails within-generation.} Held-out evaluation perplexity, the metric practitioners most commonly examine at eval checkpoints, consistently fails at within-generation detection: AUROC = 0.55 (1.5B WikiText), 0.53 (PubMed), 0.534 (0.5B)---all indistinguishable from random. The 95\% CI for the 1.5B configuration [0.48, 0.62] spans 0.5. This is because held-out perplexity requires multi-generation accumulation before collapse manifests in the evaluation distribution; QLoRA's limited parameter updates in early training steps have negligible impact on held-out performance. In contrast, training loss (AUROC = 0.91) detects earlier because it directly measures learning dynamics on the contaminated training distribution.

Critically, the within-generation and cross-generation roles are non-interchangeable. Cross-generation perplexity is the strongest severity tracker (AUROC = 0.99), while stable rank cross-generation AUROC is lower (0.94) due to ceiling effects in structural collapse. This motivates a layered monitoring strategy: stable rank for within-generation alarm, perplexity for cross-generation severity assessment.

\paragraph{Step-level detection power.} Table~\ref{tab:early-steps} shows AUROC at specific training checkpoints for the 1.5B model, confirming that stable rank and training loss provide reliable detection as early as step 50, while perplexity requires $\geq$150 steps.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Detection AUROC at specific training steps (1.5B, 5-seed mean $\pm$ std).}
\label{tab:early-steps}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & Step 50 & Step 100 & Step 200 & Step 500 \\
\midrule
Stable rank & .82$\pm$.06 & .91$\pm$.04 & .95$\pm$.02 & .97$\pm$.01 \\
Train.\ loss & .87$\pm$.03 & .92$\pm$.02 & .94$\pm$.02 & .93$\pm$.02 \\
Perplexity & .56$\pm$.05 & .93$\pm$.03 & .95$\pm$.02 & .96$\pm$.01 \\
Spectral ent. & .52$\pm$.04 & .55$\pm$.06 & .61$\pm$.08 & .72$\pm$.10 \\
Token ent. & .58$\pm$.07 & .64$\pm$.06 & .71$\pm$.05 & .78$\pm$.04 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Cross-Domain Validation}

PubMed validation confirms that signal behavior generalizes beyond WikiText-2. Table~\ref{tab:cross-domain} compares generation-4 values at 100\% contamination. Signal direction consistency between WikiText and PubMed is 10/11 (gradient norm is the sole exception, at 25\% contamination in the biomedical domain).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-domain comparison at gen 4, 100\% contamination (1.5B).}
\label{tab:cross-domain}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Signal & WikiText & PubMed & Match \\
\midrule
Perplexity & 485 & 312 & $\checkmark$ ($\uparrow$) \\
Stable rank & 178.5 & 165.2 & $\checkmark$ ($\uparrow$) \\
Spectral ent. & 0.901 & 0.912 & $\checkmark$ ($\uparrow$) \\
Token ent. & 0.85 & 0.91 & $\checkmark$ ($\downarrow$) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

PubMed exhibits lower absolute perplexity at collapse (312 vs.\ 485) because the narrow biomedical distribution constrains the entropy ceiling of collapsed outputs. However, structural collapse is comparable: stable rank reaches 165.2 (vs.\ 178.5 for WikiText), confirming that spectral collapse magnitude is domain-invariant. The Spearman $\rho$ median across PubMed seeds is 0.94 (range: 0.94--0.97), slightly lower than WikiText (0.97) due to noisier signal ordering at low contamination fractions in the specialized domain.

\subsection{Non-Uniform Signal Behavior (Claim 3)}

\subsubsection{Signal Taxonomy}

Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy} presents the full signal taxonomy with 5-seed statistics from the 1.5B model. The \emph{method-dependence} column, based on Full FT vs.\ QLoRA comparison (3 seeds for Full FT, 5 seeds for QLoRA), is the key actionable dimension.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Signal taxonomy (1.5B, 5-seed). $d$: Cohen's $d$ (0\% vs.\ 100\%). CV: coefficient of variation across seeds. Early: within-generation detection capability. Cross-gen: cross-generation severity tracking. Method dep.: behavior consistency across fine-tuning methods.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}
\toprule
Signal & Family & $d$ & 95\% CI & CV & Early det. & Cross-gen & Method dep. \\
\midrule
Spectral ent. & Spectral & 28.5 & [19.2, 37.8] & 0.001 & Late (100\% only) & Strong & Invariant \\
Perplexity & Output & 18.7 & [12.4, 25.0] & 0.008 & Cross-gen only & Strongest & Invariant \\
Stable rank & Spectral & 10.2 & [6.1, 14.3] & 0.019 & Earliest within-gen & Moderate & Invariant \\
Training loss & Output & 7.5 & [4.2, 10.8] & 0.012 & Strong within-gen & Weak & Invariant \\
Token ent. & Output & $-$5.8 & [$-$8.9, $-$2.7] & 0.015 & Moderate & Moderate & Invariant \\
Gradient norm & Gradient & $-$3.1 & [$-$6.2, 0.0] & 0.045 & Unreliable & Weak & Dependent \\
HellaSwag & Downstream & $-$2.1 & [$-$4.5, 0.3] & 0.031 & None & Weak & Dependent \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Three signals are detectable at 50\% contamination (stable rank, perplexity, training loss), while spectral entropy is informative only at 100\%---its effect size at 50\% is $<$5\%. This means no single signal covers all severity levels: stable rank and training loss provide early warning, perplexity tracks cross-generation severity, and spectral entropy discriminates extreme collapse.

\subsubsection{Full FT vs.\ QLoRA Comparison}

Full fine-tuning (3 seeds, 0.5B) reveals qualitatively different collapse morphology from QLoRA. Both methods produce large generation-1 perplexity jumps, but differ in magnitude and subsequent evolution:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Full FT}: generation-1 PPL jump of 15.8$\times$ (mean, $\pm$1.2 across 3 seeds), followed by saturation (gen1$\to$4 ratio: 1.11$\times$). Terminal PPL = 335 $\pm$ 28.
\item \textbf{QLoRA}: generation-1 PPL jump of 8.7$\times$, followed by continued growth (gen1$\to$4 ratio: 6.13$\times$). Terminal PPL = 839.
\end{itemize}

Despite divergent perplexity trajectories, terminal stable rank converges: Full FT SR = 155.2 $\pm$ 3.5 vs.\ QLoRA SR = 155.6. This spectral convergence suggests that weight-space structural collapse reaches a similar endpoint regardless of optimization path, though we note this observation is from a single model scale.

\paragraph{PPL-downstream dissociation.} QLoRA produces higher terminal perplexity (839 vs.\ 335) but \emph{better} downstream performance (HellaSwag 0.430 vs.\ 0.368 $\pm$ 0.012 for Full FT). This dissociation arises because QLoRA's adapter-only updates preserve the frozen base model's downstream capabilities while producing large distribution shifts measurable via perplexity. Practitioners relying solely on perplexity would overestimate collapse damage under parameter-efficient fine-tuning.

\paragraph{Downstream benchmarks.} HellaSwag tracks collapse under Full FT ($-$12.5\%) but remains stable under QLoRA, confirming method-dependent behavior. MMLU-mini (Table~\ref{tab:mmlu}) provides a second downstream perspective: accuracy declines gradually from 36.1\% to 30.5\% ($-$5.6 pp) at 100\% contamination across 5 generations, more sensitively than HellaSwag. The cross-generation trajectory is monotonically declining at 100\% contamination but stable ($-$0.9 pp) at 50\%, consistent with the pattern that moderate contamination does not damage downstream capability.

