\centering
\small
\caption{MMLU-mini accuracy (\%) across generations (1.5B, 5-seed mean).}
\label{tab:mmlu}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Fraction & Gen 0 & Gen 1 & Gen 2 & Gen 3 & Gen 4 \\
\midrule
0\% & 36.1 & 36.0 & 35.9 & 36.2 & 36.1 \\
50\% & 36.0 & 35.8 & 35.5 & 35.4 & 35.2 \\
100\% & 35.8 & 34.2 & 32.8 & 31.5 & 30.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Cross-Scale and Cross-Family Validation}

\paragraph{Scale trends.} Table~\ref{tab:cross-scale-full} reports generation-4 values at 100\% contamination across three Qwen2.5 scales. All four core signals maintain consistent direction. Collapse magnitude in perplexity decreases with scale (53$\times$ at 0.5B, 40$\times$ at 1.5B, 29$\times$ at 3B), while stable rank collapse magnitude increases (156, 179, 195). Cohen's $d$ for stable rank shows non-monotonic behavior across scales (9.43, 10.2, 7.8), suggesting that detection thresholds require scale-specific calibration.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale comparison at gen 4, 100\% contamination. 3B is $N$=1 (spot-check).}
\label{tab:cross-scale-full}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Metric & 0.5B & 1.5B & 3B & Dir. \\
\midrule
PPL & 839 & 485 & 310 & $\uparrow$ \\
Stable rank & 156 & 179 & 195 & $\uparrow$ \\
Spectral ent. & 0.884 & 0.901 & 0.915 & $\uparrow$ \\
Token ent. & 0.782 & 0.85 & 0.78 & $\downarrow$ \\
PPL ratio & 53$\times$ & 40$\times$ & 29$\times$ & decr. \\
$d_{\text{SR}}$ & 9.43 & 10.2 & 7.8 & non-mono. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The decreasing perplexity collapse ratio with scale reflects that larger models have greater parameter redundancy---baseline perplexity is lower (10.8 at 3B vs.\ 15.7 at 0.5B) and the model's increased capacity absorbs more corruption before catastrophic failure. At 50\% contamination, 3B PPL elevation is only 6.5\% (vs.\ 8\% at 0.5B and 9\% at 1.5B).

\paragraph{Cross-family validation.} LLaMA-3.2-1B ($N$=1) achieves 10/11 signal direction consistency with Qwen models (Table~\ref{tab:cross-family}). The sole inconsistency is gradient norm at 50\% contamination, which flips direction between architectures---supporting our characterization of gradient signals as architecture-dependent. All four core signals (stable rank, spectral entropy, perplexity, token entropy) match in direction.

