\subsection{Per-Layer Collapse Propagation}

Forward-pass SVD analysis on 1.5B checkpoints (28 layers) reveals sub-module-specific collapse patterns. Value projections (\texttt{v\_proj}) exhibit uniform stable rank diffusion across all layers ($\Delta$SR = +1.8 per layer on average), while down-projections (\texttt{down\_proj}) show localized collapse in the final three layers (L22--24, $\Delta$SR = $-$2.5). This pattern replicates at 0.5B (collapse at L11--12 of 24 layers, $\sim$80\% depth), suggesting that the proportional depth at which feed-forward collapse concentrates is scale-invariant. Table~\ref{tab:perlayer} summarizes the sub-module comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Per-sub-module mean $\Delta$SR at 100\% contamination.}
\label{tab:perlayer}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Sub-module & 0.5B $\Delta$SR & 1.5B $\Delta$SR & Pattern \\
\midrule
\texttt{v\_proj} & +1.5 & +1.8 & Uniform diffusion \\
\texttt{k\_proj} & +0.7 & +0.9 & Mild diffusion \\
\texttt{q\_proj} & +0.5 & +0.6 & Mild diffusion \\
\texttt{down\_proj} & $-$2.1 & $-$2.5 & Local collapse \\
\texttt{up\_proj} & +0.3 & +0.4 & Minimal change \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The practical takeaway: \texttt{v\_proj} stable rank is the most sensitive per-layer within-generation collapse indicator, as it responds uniformly and monotonically across all layers.

\subsection{Self-Recovery at Moderate Contamination}

At 50\% contamination, we observe partial self-recovery---signals that worsen during contaminated training partially revert in subsequent clean-data generations. Recovery prevalence is 95\% (19/20 seed$\times$signal pairs) on WikiText-2 and 80\% on PubMed. However, recovery is incomplete: the perplexity recovery ratio (proportion of damage reversed) is 0.52 $\pm$ 0.025 for WikiText and 0.40 $\pm$ 0.010 for PubMed. Table~\ref{tab:recovery} details recovery ratios by signal family.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Recovery ratio at 50\% contamination by domain and signal.}
\label{tab:recovery}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & Wiki RR & $\pm$std & PubMed RR & $\pm$std \\
\midrule
Perplexity & 0.52 & 0.025 & 0.40 & 0.010 \\
Stable rank & 0.33 & 0.045 & 0.18 & 0.095 \\
Spectral ent. & 0.31 & 0.018 & 0.15 & 0.038 \\
Token ent. & 0.59 & 0.110 & 0.44 & 0.015 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A consistent gap emerges between output-level and weight-level recovery: output metrics (perplexity, token entropy) recover more than spectral metrics (stable rank, spectral entropy), and this gap is larger for PubMed ($\Delta$ = 0.22) than WikiText ($\Delta$ = 0.19). This indicates that weight-space structural damage is more persistent than output-level degradation, and narrow-distribution domains experience less recovery. Recovery is observed only at 50\% contamination; at 25\%, degradation is too mild to constitute meaningful recovery, and at 75\%, contamination is too severe for the model to self-correct.

These observations are consistent with the data mixing effects documented by Gerstgrasser et al.\ (\citeyear{gerstgrasser2024model}): mixing real and synthetic data in appropriate proportions can partially offset collapse, and our 50\% condition represents the regime where this effect operates.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Practical monitoring recommendations.} Our results motivate a layered monitoring strategy. \emph{Within-generation}: monitor stable rank of attention value projections and training loss for early warning ($\leq$10\% training progress). \emph{Cross-generation}: track held-out perplexity for severity comparison between generations. \emph{Severity discrimination}: use spectral entropy to distinguish moderate from severe collapse. Crucially, held-out perplexity---the default monitoring metric in most training pipelines---should \emph{not} be relied upon for within-generation detection.

\paragraph{SIGMA comparison.} Preliminary data ($\sim$8 runs with SIGMA metrics collected alongside our signals) confirms directional consistency: SIGMA's effective rank increases under collapse (+1), consistent with our stable rank observations. The primary operational difference is that our training-time signals require no clean reference distribution, while SIGMA assumes access to uncorrupted outputs. A formal head-to-head comparison with sufficient statistical power remains future work.

\paragraph{Scale-dependent sensitivity.} The non-monotonic trend in stable rank Cohen's $d$ across scales (9.43 at 0.5B, 10.2 at 1.5B, 7.8 at 3B) indicates that practitioners cannot simply transfer detection thresholds across model scales. Larger models' parameter redundancy absorbs more contamination, reducing the relative amplitude of within-generation signals. This suggests that scale-specific threshold calibration is necessary for deployment.

\section{Limitations}

\begin{itemize}
\item The 3B scale experiment and LLaMA cross-family validation each use $N$=1 seed; results at these configurations should be treated as directional evidence, not statistically confirmed findings.
\item Our collapse protocol uses QLoRA and Full FT on relatively small models (0.5B--3B). Generalization to larger models, continued pre-training, or other PEFT methods (e.g., prefix tuning) is untested.
\item All experiments use 5 generations. Longer generation chains may reveal different signal dynamics, particularly for signals that saturate early (Full FT perplexity).
\item Lexical diversity metrics (distinct-$n$, self-BLEU) show minimal sensitivity to collapse in our setting and are not included in the taxonomy as effective monitors.
\item We study detection rather than mitigation. Whether the early warning signals enable effective intervention (e.g., data filtering, early stopping) remains future work.
\item SIGMA comparison is preliminary ($\sim$8 data points); formal head-to-head requires dedicated experimental runs.
\item Recovery prevalence comparisons across experiments with different signal sets should be interpreted cautiously, as the selected signals may not be representative of all possible monitoring metrics.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

We present a systematic empirical analysis of training-time signals as model collapse severity monitors. Across model scales (0.5B--3B), text domains (WikiText-2, PubMed), model families (Qwen, LLaMA), and fine-tuning methods (QLoRA, Full FT), we establish three findings: (1) spectral and output signals reliably track collapse severity with monotonic ordering (Spearman $\rho$ median = 0.97 over 5 seeds); (2) stable rank provides the earliest within-generation warning (AUROC = 0.97), while held-out perplexity fails at this task (AUROC = 0.55) despite being the most commonly monitored metric; (3) signal informativeness depends on training method and severity level, requiring practitioners to adopt a multi-signal monitoring strategy. Our signal taxonomy provides actionable guidance: stable rank for within-generation alarm, perplexity for cross-generation tracking, and spectral entropy for severity discrimination---no single signal suffices.

