\end{table}

Stable rank is the strongest within-generation detector across all three configurations. At just 10\% training progress, stable rank achieves sensitivity 0.82 and specificity 0.97 on the 1.5B model. Training loss---which directly measures model behavior on the contaminated distribution---is the second-best within-generation signal (AUROC = 0.91).

\paragraph{Held-out perplexity fails within-generation.} Held-out evaluation perplexity, the metric practitioners most commonly examine at eval checkpoints, consistently fails at within-generation detection: AUROC = 0.55 (1.5B WikiText), 0.53 (PubMed), 0.534 (0.5B)---all indistinguishable from random. The 95\% CI for the 1.5B configuration [0.48, 0.62] spans 0.5. This is because held-out perplexity requires multi-generation accumulation before collapse manifests in the evaluation distribution; QLoRA's limited parameter updates in early training steps have negligible impact on held-out performance. In contrast, training loss (AUROC = 0.91) detects earlier because it directly measures learning dynamics on the contaminated training distribution.

Critically, the within-generation and cross-generation roles are non-interchangeable. Cross-generation perplexity is the strongest severity tracker (AUROC = 0.99), while stable rank cross-generation AUROC is lower (0.94) due to ceiling effects in structural collapse. This motivates a layered monitoring strategy: stable rank for within-generation alarm, perplexity for cross-generation severity assessment.

\paragraph{Step-level detection power.} Table~\ref{tab:early-steps} shows AUROC at specific training checkpoints for the 1.5B model, confirming that stable rank and training loss provide reliable detection as early as step 50, while perplexity requires $\geq$150 steps.

