PubMed exhibits lower absolute perplexity at collapse (312 vs.\ 485) because the narrow biomedical distribution constrains the entropy ceiling of collapsed outputs. However, structural collapse is comparable: stable rank reaches 165.2 (vs.\ 178.5 for WikiText), confirming that spectral collapse magnitude is domain-invariant. The Spearman $\rho$ median across PubMed seeds is 0.94 (range: 0.94--0.97), slightly lower than WikiText (0.97) due to noisier signal ordering at low contamination fractions in the specialized domain.

\subsection{Non-Uniform Signal Behavior (Claim 3)}

\subsubsection{Signal Taxonomy}

Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy} presents the full signal taxonomy with 5-seed statistics from the 1.5B model. The \emph{method-dependence} column, based on Full FT vs.\ QLoRA comparison (3 seeds for Full FT, 5 seeds for QLoRA), is the key actionable dimension.

