Three signals are detectable at 50\% contamination (stable rank, perplexity, training loss), while spectral entropy is informative only at 100\%---its effect size at 50\% is $<$5\%. This means no single signal covers all severity levels: stable rank and training loss provide early warning, perplexity tracks cross-generation severity, and spectral entropy discriminates extreme collapse.

\subsubsection{Full FT vs.\ QLoRA Comparison}

Full fine-tuning (3 seeds, 0.5B) reveals qualitatively different collapse morphology from QLoRA. Both methods produce large generation-1 perplexity jumps, but differ in magnitude and subsequent evolution:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Full FT}: generation-1 PPL jump of 15.8$\times$ (mean, $\pm$1.2 across 3 seeds), followed by saturation (gen1$\to$4 ratio: 1.11$\times$). Terminal PPL = 335 $\pm$ 28.
\item \textbf{QLoRA}: generation-1 PPL jump of 8.7$\times$, followed by continued growth (gen1$\to$4 ratio: 6.13$\times$). Terminal PPL = 839.
\end{itemize}

Despite divergent perplexity trajectories, terminal stable rank converges: Full FT SR = 155.2 $\pm$ 3.5 vs.\ QLoRA SR = 155.6. This spectral convergence suggests that weight-space structural collapse reaches a similar endpoint regardless of optimization path, though we note this observation is from a single model scale.

\paragraph{PPL-downstream dissociation.} QLoRA produces higher terminal perplexity (839 vs.\ 335) but \emph{better} downstream performance (HellaSwag 0.430 vs.\ 0.368 $\pm$ 0.012 for Full FT). This dissociation arises because QLoRA's adapter-only updates preserve the frozen base model's downstream capabilities while producing large distribution shifts measurable via perplexity. Practitioners relying solely on perplexity would overestimate collapse damage under parameter-efficient fine-tuning.

\paragraph{Downstream benchmarks.} HellaSwag tracks collapse under Full FT ($-$12.5\%) but remains stable under QLoRA, confirming method-dependent behavior. MMLU-mini (Table~\ref{tab:mmlu}) provides a second downstream perspective: accuracy declines gradually from 36.1\% to 30.5\% ($-$5.6 pp) at 100\% contamination across 5 generations, more sensitively than HellaSwag. The cross-generation trajectory is monotonically declining at 100\% contamination but stable ($-$0.9 pp) at 50\%, consistent with the pattern that moderate contamination does not damage downstream capability.

